naught  after we know this we start to challenge  ourselves with much more complicated  problem such as bc reaction  bz reaction is a really complicated  chemical reaction model and its  simplified pde equations can be seen  here the simplified pde equations has a  rational term which cannot be identified  using pdefined before  so what we did is we simulated the  simulation  data  in the matlab and we provided the  simulation data to this indie pi and the  cindy pi correctly identifies this  rational and implicit equation and also  the remaining dynamics of the system  and when we simulate this identified  equation  in matlab we find out it matches really  good with the ground truth  the second example we looked at is  double pendulum so double pendulum looks  pretty easy right it only has two links  but actually double pendulum is a really  chaotic and non-linear system moreover  it has a pretty nasty rational terms  pretty complicated rational ode  so we simulated double pendulum in  matlab with different noise level and we  provided those data set to cindy pi  and we find out cindy pi can correctly  identify the odes of double pendulum  under different noise level and when we  simulate this identified odes forward in  time it matches really good with the  ground tools  all right the next examples we used is a  system with control input if you're a  control expert who is interested in  dynamical system with control input the  cindy pi can also tackle that  for example the single pendulum with  control  input it has a rational term in its ode  so when we simulated the system in  matlab under different noise level and  we provided the simulation data to cindy  pi we find out cindy pi can generate the  correct model and when we simulate this  correct model uh it matches really good  with the ground rules  and as our final examples we even tried  even more challenging tasks for example  identifying physical loss from the data  set  many physical laws such as hamiltonian  or lagrangian actually has an implicit  form for example the double pendulum's  euler lagrangian equation has an  implicit form so when we simulate the  double pendulum and building the correct  libraries we find out the cindy pie can  figure out the correct physical loss  that's hidden beneath this data set and  in this case the cindy pi correctly  identified all the lagrangian equation  from the from the simulation data of  double pendulum  okay  now in summary we did develop a new  algorithm called zindipi this algorithm  can parallelly solve for implicit or  rational problem and moreover it is more  noise robust and requires less data  point  finally it can identify physical laws  from the data  if you're interested in other  papers please feel free to check it out  and if you want to use cindy pi for your  research at work please also check out  the packages below  and i would like to thank my  collaborators professor steve ronton and  professor nissan quits at university of  washington and thank you all for  listening
 

